```

In this example:
- `Dashboard` and `Settings` components are lazily loaded.
- `Suspense` is used to provide a fallback UI while the components are being loaded.
- The components are conditionally rendered based on the current route.

### 3. Intersection Observer API

You can use the Intersection Observer API to detect when a component comes into view and load it only when it is visible.

#### Example:

```jsx
import React, { useState, useEffect, lazy, Suspense } from 'react';

// Lazily load the Dashboard component
const Dashboard = lazy(() => import('./Dashboard'));

function App() {
  const [isLoaded, setIsLoaded] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
      ([entry]) => {
        if (entry.isIntersecting) {
          setIsLoaded(true);
          observer.unobserve(entry.target);
        }
      },
      { threshold: 0.1 }
    );

    const target = document.getElementById('dashboard-target');
    if (target) {
      observer.observe(target);
    }

    return () => {
      if (target) {
        observer.unobserve(target);
      }
    };
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      <div id="dashboard-target" style={{ height: '100vh' }}></div>
      {isLoaded && (
        <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>
          <Dashboard />
        </Suspense>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}

export default App;
```

In this example:
- An `IntersectionObserver` is used to detect when the `#dashboard-target` element comes into view.
- When the element is intersecting, the `Dashboard` component is loaded.
- The `Suspense` component provides a fallback UI while the `Dashboard` component is being loaded.

### Summary

By using `React.lazy` and `Suspense`, you can defer the loading of components until they are needed, which can significantly improve the initial load time and overall performance of your application. Additionally, conditional rendering and the Intersection Observer API can be used to further optimize the loading of components based on user interaction and visibility.

